    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Get into position safely: sit on the end of the bench with the dumbbells on the thighs, then lie back and kick them up one knee at a time; at the end of the set bring them back to the thighs or lower them to the floor beside the bench rather than dropping them (clinical opinion).",
        "Shoulder pain: shorten the range at the bottom and keep the upper arms out of the fully stretched position until it settles (clinical opinion).",
        "Keep the wrists straight over the forearms; bent-back wrists under a heavy dumbbell strain the wrist.",
        "Stop if sharp pain, numbness or tingling occurs.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Shoulder discomfort: use a neutral grip (palms facing each other) and tuck the elbows a little more.",
        "Lower back arching too much: put the feet on a low step or the end of the bench."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Floor press with dumbbells (the floor limits the depth).",
        "Push-up or incline push-up."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Heavier dumbbells.",
        "Slower lowering (3-4 s).",
        "Single-arm dumbbell press (adds a trunk anti-rotation demand)."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "pec_r": "Main pusher: horizontal adduction and flexion of the upper arm. On a flat bench the middle (sternal) and lower (costal) fibres were most active (Rodríguez-Ridao 2020, barbell), and pectoralis activity was similar for dumbbell, barbell and Smith machine presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "pec_l": "Main pusher: horizontal adduction and flexion of the upper arm. On a flat bench the middle (sternal) and lower (costal) fibres were most active (Rodríguez-Ridao 2020, barbell), and pectoralis activity was similar for dumbbell, barbell and Smith machine presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "ant_deltoid_r": "Flexes the shoulder with the chest. Its activity did not differ between dumbbell and barbell presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "ant_deltoid_l": "Flexes the shoulder with the chest. Its activity did not differ between dumbbell and barbell presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "triceps_r": "Straightens the elbows in the top half; triceps activity was lower with dumbbells than with a barbell or Smith machine (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "triceps_l": "Straightens the elbows in the top half; triceps activity was lower with dumbbells than with a barbell or Smith machine (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "serratus_r": "Holds the shoulder blades against the rib cage while each arm moves on its own.",
      "serratus_l": "Holds the shoulder blades against the rib cage while each arm moves on its own.",
      "biceps_r": "Works as a stabiliser: biceps activity rose with the stability demand, highest with dumbbells (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "biceps_l": "Works as a stabiliser: biceps activity rose with the stability demand, highest with dumbbells (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "forearm_flexors_r": "Grip the dumbbell handles.",
      "forearm_flexors_l": "Grip the dumbbell handles.",
      "infraspinatus_r": "Rotator cuff: centres the head of the upper arm while the dumbbells balance freely (clinical opinion).",
      "infraspinatus_l": "Rotator cuff: centres the head of the upper arm while the dumbbells balance freely (clinical opinion).",
      "rhomboids_midtrap_r": "Keep the shoulder blades drawn back and down on the bench.",
      "rhomboids_midtrap_l": "Keep the shoulder blades drawn back and down on the bench.",
      "core": "Bracing keeps the ribs down and the pelvis on the seat."
    }
  },
